  "source": "Treasury MYEFO / Budget",
  "source_url": "https://budget.gov.au",
  "description": "Australian Federal Government net debt as a percentage of GDP. Net debt = gross debt minus financial assets held by government (cash, investments). Expressed as % of GDP for comparability across time.",
  "explainer": "Net debt subtracts government financial assets (superannuation fund investments, cash holdings) from gross borrowings \u2014 giving a more accurate picture of the government's true liability. Expressing it as % of GDP adjusts for the fact that a larger economy can carry more debt. Australia ran net surpluses (negative net debt) through the Howard years, then took on significant debt post-GFC (stimulus spending) and again post-Covid. At around 22% of GDP, Australia's net debt is low by international standards \u2014 the US is above 100%, Japan above 160%.",
  "projection_source": "Treasury Budget 2025\u201326 and Medium Term Projections. Projects net debt peaking around 24% of GDP before gradually declining.",
